To find words surrounded by mustache syntax in a text using regular expressions (regex), you can use the following pattern. Mustache syntax typically includes the following parts:
- `{{` and `}}` for the opening and closing tags.

Here's a regex pattern to match words surrounded by these tags:

```regex
\b\{(?:\s*[^{}]+)\}\b
```

Explanation:
- `\b`: Word boundary to ensure we match whole words.
- `\{`: Matches the opening curly brace.
- `(?:\s*[^{}]+)`: Non-capturing group to match optional whitespace followed by one or more characters that are not `{` or `}`.
- `\}\b`: Matches the closing curly brace and ensures it's at a word boundary.

Here’s how you can use this regex in different programming languages:

### Python
```python
import re

text = "This is {{example}} and {{example}} in the {{mustache}} syntax."
pattern = r'\b\{(?:\s*[^{}]+)\}\b'
matches = re.findall(pattern, text)
print(matches)  # Output: ['{{example}}', '{{example}}', '{{mustache}}']
```

### JavaScript
```javascript
const text = "This is {{example}} and {{example}} in the {{mustache}} syntax.";
const pattern = /\b\{(?:\s*[^{}]+)\}\b/g;
const matches = text.match(pattern);
console.log(matches);  // Output: ['{{example}}', '{{example}}', '{{mustache}}']
```

### Java
```java
import java.util.regex.*;
import java.util.*;

public class MustacheRegex {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "This is {{example}} and {{example}} in the {{mustache}} syntax.";
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("\\b\\{(?:\\s*[^{}]+)\\}\\b");
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(text);
        List<String> matches = new ArrayList<>();
        while (matcher.find()) {
            matches.add(matcher.group());
        }
        System.out.println(matches);  // Output: [{{example}}, {{example}}, {{mustache}]
    }
}
```

### Ruby
```ruby
text = "This is {{example}} and {{example}} in the {{mustache}} syntax."
matches = text.scan(/\b\{(?:\s*[^{}]+)\}\b/)
puts matches.inspect  # Output: ["{{example}}", "{{example}}", "{{mustache}}"]
```

### C#
```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string text = "This is {{example}} and {{example}} in the {{mustache}} syntax.";
        Regex pattern = new Regex(@"\b\{(?:\s*[^{}]+)\}\b");
        MatchCollection matches = pattern.Matches(text);
        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
        }
    }
}
```

This regex pattern is designed to match words that are enclosed within the curly braces, ensuring that it correctly handles cases where the word might be part of a larger expression or surrounded by whitespace. Adjustments might be needed based on the specific requirements of the text being processed.

