Creating a complex smart contract involves several steps, including defining the contract's purpose, writing the code, testing, and deploying it on a blockchain platform like Ethereum. Below is a simplified example of how to create a basic smart contract that manages tokens and transfers ownership. This example will be written using Solidity, the programming language for Ethereum smart contracts.

### Step 1: Define the Contract

First, define the purpose of the smart contract. In this case, we will create a contract that allows users to mint new tokens and transfer ownership of existing tokens.

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract TokenOwnership {
    struct Token {
        uint256 id;
        address owner;
    }

    mapping(uint256 => Token) public tokens;
    mapping(address => uint256) public ownerIndex;

    event Transfer(uint256 indexed tokenId, address indexed from, address indexed to);

    constructor() {
        // Initialization code can go here
    }

    // Function to mint a new token
    function mintToken(uint256 _tokenId) public {
        require(ownerIndex[msg.sender] == 0, "You already own a token.");
        tokens[_tokenId] = Token(_tokenId, msg.sender);
        ownerIndex[msg.sender] = _tokenId;
        emit Transfer(_tokenId, address(0), msg.sender);
    }

    // Function to transfer ownership
    function transferOwnership(uint256 _tokenId, address _newOwner) public {
        require(msg.sender == ownerIndex[_tokenId], "You do not own the token.");
        ownerIndex[_tokenId] = _newOwner;
        emit Transfer(_tokenId, tokens[_tokenId].owner, _newOwner);
    }

    // Function to get token details
    function getToken(uint256 _tokenId) public view returns (Token memory) {
        return tokens[_tokenId];
    }

    // Function to get the owner of a token
    function getOwner(uint256 _tokenId) public view returns (address) {
        return ownerIndex[_tokenId];
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Compile the Contract

After writing your smart contract, you need to compile it using a tool like Truffle or Remix. This step involves generating bytecode and ABI (Application Binary Interface) for your contract.

### Step 3: Deploy the Contract

Once compiled, you can deploy your contract on a blockchain platform like Ethereum. This can be done using a wallet like MetaMask or a deployment tool like Truffle.

### Step 4: Write Smart Contract Code

Here's a more complex example that demonstrates a more sophisticated contract, such as a contract that manages a game where players can buy and sell items.

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract ItemMarket {
    struct Item {
        string name;
        uint256 price;
        uint256 itemId;
    }

    mapping(uint256 => Item) public items;
    mapping(uint256 => address) public seller;

    event Sale(uint256 indexed itemId, address indexed buyer, address indexed seller);

    constructor() {
        // Initialization code can go here
    }

    // Function to add an item
    function addItem(string memory _name, uint256 _price) public {
        items[items.length + 1] = Item(_name, _price, items.length + 1);
        seller[items.length + 1] = msg.sender;
        emit Sale(items.length + 1, msg.sender, msg.sender);
    }

    // Function to buy an item
    function buyItem(uint256 _itemId, address _buyer) public payable {
        require(seller[_itemId] == msg.sender, "You are not the seller.");
        uint256 price = items[_itemId].price;
        require(msg.value == price, "Insufficient funds.");
        items[_itemId] = Item("", 0, 0);
        seller[_itemId] = address(0);
        emit Sale(_itemId, _buyer, msg.sender);
    }

    // Function to get item details
    function getItem(uint256 _itemId) public view returns (Item memory) {
        return items[_itemId];
    }

    // Function to get the seller of an item
    function getSeller(uint256 _itemId) public view returns (address) {
        return seller[_itemId];
    }
}
```

### Step 5: Test the Contract

Before deploying, test your contract thoroughly using tools like Truffle or Remix to ensure it functions as expected.

### Step 6: Deploy the Contract

Deploy the contract to a blockchain network. Ensure that the contract is accessible and can be interacted with by users.

### Step 7: Monitor and Update

Monitor the contract for any issues and update it as needed to reflect changes in the game mechanics or functionality.

### Step 8: Secure

Ensure the contract is secure by implementing proper error handling, input validation, and following best practices for smart contract security.

### Conclusion

Creating a complex smart contract requires careful planning, thorough testing, and adherence to best practices. This example provides a basic structure for creating token management and item trading contracts. For more complex contracts, consider incorporating features like escrow, auctions, or more sophisticated game mechanics.

